// The `bit_rt_random_bytes` ABI wrapper (ABI.md §21) over this directory's
// Linux entropy source (#1626). Sibling of `entropy.bit`, so `entropyFill` is
// a module-scoped call, not a cross-module reference.
//
// WHY THIS WRAPPER IS DUPLICATED PER PLATFORM rather than living once in the
// portable `../rand.bit`. The wrapper's only platform-dependent part is which
// `entropyFill` it reaches, and Bit has no OS-conditional compilation: a single
// portable file would have to `import` one of `./linux` or `./darwin` by a
// literal path, which is a compile-time choice it cannot make. So the wrapper
// sits beside each entropy source, exactly the shape SEAM 1 (#1600) settled on
// for the 12 `bit_rt_root_net_*` wrappers after the `extern`-from-the-parent
// form failed the Linux emit. The twin lives in `../darwin/random.bit` and is
// identical but for this comment and the entropy source it compiles against.
//
// SORTS AFTER `entropy.bit`. Module files concatenate in bytewise filename
// order and the inliner only inlines a callee it has already lowered (#1511),
// so `random.bit` must sort after the file defining `entropyFill` — `e` < `r`.
//
// PIN CARRIES THE `bit_rt_root_` PREFIX: `runtime/root.zig` is still linked into
// every binary and already owns `bit_rt_random_bytes`, so claiming the real name
// now fails every link with DuplicateSymbol. G2 (#1583) drops the `_root` infix.
//
// `rtStrAlloc` is root's pinned string allocator (`bit_rt_port_root_str_alloc`)
// and `strPtr` its object-layout constant (ABI.md §2); `loadWord` is `runtime/gc`'s
// pinned raw-memory glue. All three are pinned/compile-time, so the freestanding
// emit resolves them without an unpinned mangled reference (#1612).
import { rtStrAlloc, strPtr } from "../../root"
import { loadWord } from "../../gc"

// `bit_rt_random_bytes` (ABI.md §21): a fresh `string` of `len`
// cryptographically-secure bytes from the OS CSPRNG. `root.zig`'s
// `bit_rt_random_bytes`, decision for decision.
//
// A non-positive `len` yields the empty string (mirroring `fs_read`), and an
// entropy failure is FATAL — this never hands back weak or zero bytes, the
// policy `entropy.bit` documents and the Zig's
// `catch fatal("random_bytes: OS CSPRNG failed")` enforces. `fatal` is
// `panicWrite` + exit 2 (root.zig:459), i.e. exactly `bit_rt_panic`, which is
// what the `panic` builtin lowers to. That is not a pin cycle: this module
// defines no `bit_rt_root_panic`, so no rename can make the call self-referential
// (the property tests/rootpins.zig proves per symbol).
//
// FILLS THE PACKED PAYLOAD, not a `[]u8`: the bytes of a `string` object are the
// inline region `strPtr` addresses, and only a raw `*u8` can name it — a `[]u8`
// is word-strided (one 8-byte word per element) and `entropyFill` writes packed
// bytes straight from the kernel.
export @symbol("bit_rt_root_random_bytes") function rtRandomBytes(len: int): int {
  if (len <= 0) {
    return rtStrAlloc(0)
  }
  let s = rtStrAlloc(len)
  if (s == 0) {
    return 0 // heap exhausted; the `bit_rt_string_from_bytes` convention
  }
  let nilb: *u8 = nil
  let p = nilb + loadWord(s + strPtr)
  if (!entropyFill(p, len)) {
    panic("random_bytes: OS CSPRNG failed")
  }
  return s
}
